00:07:51.028 --> 00:07:56.408
Pulling several forecasts is how it works when a published probability is built from more

00:07:56.408 --> 00:08:01.028
than one source, and the research keeps finding that an equal weight average beats the mix

00:08:01.028 --> 00:08:02.328
someone tried to optimize.

00:08:02.868 --> 00:08:07.028
That result has a name in the literature, the forecast combination puzzle.

00:08:07.508 --> 00:08:12.428
The ordinary build is a weighted average, each forecaster, pole or model gets a weight,

00:08:12.848 --> 00:08:15.328
and the weights are picked so old errors get smaller.

00:08:15.868 --> 00:08:19.548
The plain split of the same forecasts is what keeps winning on robustness.

00:08:19.548 --> 00:08:23.488
A 2015 paper on archive looks at three reasons.

00:08:23.968 --> 00:08:25.388
Wates are estimated badly.

00:08:25.868 --> 00:08:30.408
The waiting formula does not fit the data, or models get screened out before the pool

00:08:30.408 --> 00:08:31.308
is even formed.

00:08:31.788 --> 00:08:34.248
The paper adds a split that earlier write-up skipped.

00:08:34.888 --> 00:08:39.628
Combining for adaptation means you do not know which single forecast will be best, so you

00:08:39.628 --> 00:08:41.968
want a blend that stays near whichever one wins.

00:08:42.608 --> 00:08:46.828
Combining for improvement means you think the blend can beat every candidate, including

00:08:46.828 --> 00:08:47.608
the best one.

00:08:47.608 --> 00:08:51.828
Use the wrong one of those two jobs, and you can manufacture the puzzle yourself.

00:08:52.348 --> 00:08:55.628
The fix that paper offers is a multi-level after strategy.

00:08:56.248 --> 00:08:57.608
After is sequential waiting.

00:08:58.228 --> 00:09:02.908
The multi-level version treats the simple average as just another candidate, so the scheme

00:09:02.908 --> 00:09:07.648
can drop back to equal weights when the estimated ones are too noisy, and it can still lean

00:09:07.648 --> 00:09:10.368
on a strong forecast when the record supports the lean.

00:09:10.988 --> 00:09:16.468
A 2023 paper, also on archive, says part of the puzzle is the test, not the forecast.

00:09:16.468 --> 00:09:23.368
The way these combinations are usually produced leaves accuracy tests with low power and without proper size control.

00:09:24.048 --> 00:09:31.048
Under a null of no inferior accuracy, the test statistic does not follow the standard normal distribution people usually assume.

00:09:31.628 --> 00:09:34.088
The asymptotic distribution is non-standard.

00:09:34.568 --> 00:09:38.028
A test that is too quick to bless the simple average will keep blessing it.

00:09:38.528 --> 00:09:44.568
That paper says the weak power can be avoided when the data allow it by estimating the combinations more efficiently.

00:09:44.568 --> 00:09:49.468
It shows this for a functional of interest and for predictive densities, and it walks

00:09:49.468 --> 00:09:54.348
a short example on daily financial returns, so a researcher can see how to step around

00:09:54.348 --> 00:09:54.808
the puzzle.

00:09:55.548 --> 00:10:00.568
Probability forecasts break in a further way, and that one is in the journal of the Royal Statistical

00:10:00.568 --> 00:10:01.808
Society in 2010.

00:10:02.608 --> 00:10:06.808
Linear pooling, a weighted average of probabilities, is the method people reach for.

00:10:07.288 --> 00:10:11.888
Start with two or more distinct forecasts that are each calibrated, meaning events happen

00:10:11.888 --> 00:10:13.308
at the rate the forecast names.

00:10:13.308 --> 00:10:18.928
times. Any weighted average that is not trivial is then necessarily uncalibrated, and it lacks

00:10:18.928 --> 00:10:24.088
sharpness. The pool gets pulled in toward the middle relative to the sharper input. You

00:10:24.088 --> 00:10:29.448
need a recalibration even in that ideal case. The tool in that paper is a beta-transformed

00:10:29.448 --> 00:10:34.288
linear opinion pool. A beta curve, fit along with the weights, can push the pooled number

00:10:34.288 --> 00:10:39.748
out toward zero or one, or pull it back in after the linear average is done. The authors

00:10:39.748 --> 00:10:43.568
Players show it in a simulation and in a case study that pairs statistical forecasts

00:10:43.568 --> 00:10:46.528
with national weather service probabilities of precipitation.

00:10:47.228 --> 00:10:51.428
A 2021 paper on archive takes the reason Ensemble sit on a shelf.

00:10:52.128 --> 00:10:56.688
Dynamic combinations, the kind that move the weights as the series moves, can forecast

00:10:56.688 --> 00:11:01.068
well and still never ship because they are expensive to run and hard to explain.

00:11:01.688 --> 00:11:04.788
Model compression trains a single model to imitate the stack.

00:11:05.308 --> 00:11:09.428
In those experiments, the compressed model matched the Ensemble closely enough to call

00:11:09.428 --> 00:11:12.988
the performance comparable, and the computing cost fell drastically.

00:11:13.588 --> 00:11:18.528
The compressed model with the best average rank was a rule-based regression, so the forecast

00:11:18.528 --> 00:11:20.068
also got easier to read.

00:11:20.548 --> 00:11:24.808
Time series make that compression harder than the neural network cases the field usually

00:11:24.808 --> 00:11:27.528
studies, because the data do not sit still.

00:11:27.988 --> 00:11:31.208
A market price is already a pool of other people's probabilities.

00:11:31.808 --> 00:11:36.828
Set it next to a poll, a model and a second venue, and an equal weight average is the blend

00:11:36.828 --> 00:11:41.888
this research treats as the sturdy default when you are adapting and you do not have a thick sample

00:11:41.888 --> 00:11:47.228
for estimating weights. Estimated weights have a job when you are trying to improve on every input,

00:11:47.748 --> 00:11:52.268
and you can estimate them without spending the gain on the estimation itself. A straight

00:11:52.268 --> 00:11:57.768
average of calibrated probabilities is not a calibrated probability. It wants that beta-style

00:11:57.768 --> 00:12:02.908
recalibration before anyone treats the blend as sharp. Knowing that changes how you read a

00:12:02.908 --> 00:12:07.668
published number. It is a pool, the pooling rule changes the number, and none of this

00:12:07.668 --> 00:12:12.448
is a method for picking a contract. That's prediction markets daily for today. Nothing

